hich again gives: L + R = T + B, same as Equation 1.\n\nNow, since L + R = T + B, and L + R + T + B = (L + R) + (T + B) = 2*(L + R) = total sum minus Y.\n\nTotal sum = L + R + T + B + Y = 25\n\nSo: 2*(L + R) + Y = 25\n\nTherefore: 2*(L + R) = 25 - Y\n\nSo 25 - Y must be even, meaning Y must be odd.\n\nThe numbers available are: 3, 9, 5, 8, 0.\n\nOdd numbers: 3, 9, 5.\n\nEven numbers: 8, 0.\n\nSo Y (the yellow circle) must be one of 3, 9, or 5.\n\nThat eliminates 8 and 0.\n\nNow, let\u2019s check which of these can actually be placed in the center.\n\nWe need to check if for each of 3, 9, 5, we can assign the remaining four numbers to the other four positions such that L + R = T + B.\n\nLet\u2019s try Y = 3.\n\nThen 2*(L + R) = 25 - 3 = 22 \u2192 L + R = 11\n\nRemaining numbers: 9, 5, 8, 0\n\nWe need to split them into two pairs: (L, R) and (T, B) such that L + R = 11 and T + B = 11.\n\nPossible pairs that sum to 11 from {9,5,8,0}:\n\n- 9 + 2? No 2.\n- 8 + 3? 3 is used.\n- 5 + 6? No 6.\n- 0 + 11? No.\n- 9 + 0 = 9\n- 9 + 5 = 14\n- 9 + 8 = 17\n- 9 + 0 = 9\n- 8 + 5 = 13\n- 8 + 0 = 8\n- 5 + 0 = 5\n\nNone of these pairs sum to 11.\n\nWait, let's list all possible pairs from the set {9,5,8,0}:\n\nPossible pairs:\n\n- 9+5=14\n- 9+8=17\n- 9+0=9\n- 5+8=13\n- 5+0=5\n- 8+0=8\n\nNone sum to 11.\n\nSo Y cannot be 3.\n\nNow try Y = 9.\n\nThen 2*(L + R) = 25 - 9 = 16 \u2192 L + R = 8\n\nRemaining numbers: 3, 5, 8, 0\n\nWe need two pairs that each sum to 8.\n\nPossible pairs:\n\n- 3+5=8 \u2192 yes!\n- 8+0=8 \u2192 yes!\n\nPerfect.\n\nSo we can assign:\n\nOne pair to (L, R): say L=3, R=5\n\nOther pair to (T, B): T=8, B=0\n\nOr L=5, R=3; T=8, B=0\n\nOr L=3, R=5; T=0, B=8\n\nOr L=5, R=3; T=0, B=8\n\nAll work.\n\nSo Y=9 is possible.\n\nNow try Y=5.\n\nThen 2*(L + R) = 25 - 5 = 20 \u2192 L + R = 10\n\nRemaining numbers: 3, 9, 8, 0\n\nWe need two pairs that each sum to 10.\n\nPossible pairs:\n\n- 3+9=12\n- 3+8=11\n- 3+0=3\n- 9+8=17\n- 9+0=9\n- 8+0=8\n\nNone sum to 10.\n\nSo Y cannot be 5.